[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Application services on the Server Room host pc1 (lan_servers). Both the web service (Apache HTTP) and the file-sharing service were not running on the server.

Mechanism: From CS lab hosts we could ping the server (ICMP OK), but TCP connections to application ports were refused, meaning nothing was listening. Initially, cs_lab1->pc1 netcat to ports 80/445/139 all returned “Connection refused.” On pc1, netstat showed no listeners except 127.0.0.11; systemctl reported apache2 “enabled” but “inactive (dead)”, and smbd/nmbd units were “not found.” After starting apache2 on pc1, port 80 began listening and cs_lab1 could connect to 80; ports 445/139 remain refused.

Localization: Single device pc1 (1.0.0.5) in the Server Room, process level (apache2 inactive; Samba absent). Routing/L2 across CS LAN to Server Room is healthy.

Root cause: Services on the server were stopped/removed this afternoon—apache2 was not running, and Samba binaries/units are missing—so application endpoints disappeared despite network reachability.

Corrective fix: Ensure services are installed and enabled on pc1. Actions: 
- Web: enable and keep apache2 running (systemctl enable --now apache2; consider Restart=always). 
- File shares: install and configure Samba (apt-get install samba), restore share configuration, then systemctl enable --now smbd (and nmbd if NetBIOS required). 
Verify from a CS lab host that ports 80 and 445 are listening and accessible, and add monitoring to alert if these daemons stop again.
=== END ===